{"name":"Witches Cauldron","hex":"#35343f","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Witches Cauldron","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A basin in Douglas Range, Alexander Island, Bellingshausen Sea, Southern Ocean, off the coast of the Antarctic Peninsula, Antarctica; an ice-filled basin found near Mount Egbert."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Witches_Cauldron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Witches Cauldron (Antarctica)","blocks":[{"text":"Witches Cauldron is an ice-filled basin on the west side of the Douglas Range, immediately west of Mount Egbert in the north part of Alexander Island, Antarctica. The basin was first sighted from the air and roughly mapped by the British Graham Land expedition in 1937. More accurately mapped from air photos taken by the Ronne Antarctic Research Expedition in 1947–48, by Searle of the Falkland Islands Dependencies Survey in 1960. Named by the UK Antarctic Place-Names Committee for the feature's kettle-like shape."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witches_Cauldron_(Antarctica)","wikidata":"Q8027919"},{"title":"Witchcraft","blocks":[{"text":"Witchcraft is the use of magic by a person called a witch. Traditionally, \"witchcraft\" means the use of magic to inflict supernatural harm or misfortune on others, and this remains the most common and widespread meaning. Though the idea of witchcraft is largely imaginary, it has nevertheless served in many cultures as a way to explain the presence of evil. The belief in witches has been found throughout history in a great number of societies worldwide. Most of these societies have used protective magic or counter-magic against witchcraft, and have shunned, banished, imprisoned, physically punished or killed alleged witches. Anthropologists use the term \"witchcraft\" for similar beliefs about harmful occult practices in different cultures, and these societies often use the term when speaking in English."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/John_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG/330px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witchcraft","wikidata":"Q259745"},{"title":"Cauldron","blocks":[{"text":"A cauldron is a large pot or kettle used for cooking or boiling, typically over an open fire. It often features a lid, an arc-shaped hanger, and/or integral handles or feet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Gulyas080.jpg/330px-Gulyas080.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cauldron","wikidata":"Q1317634"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/John_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/John_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of witchcraft in John William Waterhouse's painting The Magic Circle (1886)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_William_Waterhouse_-_Magic_Circle.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Witchcraft%3B_a_witch_and_a_devil_making_a_nail_with_which_to_Wellcome_V0025812EBL.jpg/500px-Witchcraft%3B_a_witch_and_a_devil_making_a_nail_with_which_to_Wellcome_V0025812EBL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Witchcraft%3B_a_witch_and_a_devil_making_a_nail_with_which_to_Wellcome_V0025812EBL.jpg/1280px-Witchcraft%3B_a_witch_and_a_devil_making_a_nail_with_which_to_Wellcome_V0025812EBL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn